{"id": 1924823, "name": "DESCHAMPS v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO.", "name_abbreviation": "DesChamps v. Atlantic Coast Line R. R.", "decision_date": "1909-04-09", "docket_number": "7124", "first_page": "236", "last_page": "238", "citations": [{"type": "official", "cite": "82 S.C. 236"}], "court": {"name_abbreviation": "S.C.", "id": 8894, "name": "Supreme Court of South Carolina"}, "jurisdiction": {"id": 59, "name_long": "South Carolina", "name": "S.C."}, "cites_to": [{"cite": "77 S. C., 76", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [1915524], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/77/0076-01"]}, {"cite": "76 S. C., 13", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "50 S. C., 129", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4408100], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/50/0129-01"]}, {"cite": "10 L- R. A., 749", "category": "reporters:federal", "reporter": "L.R.A.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "60 S- C., 293", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [8715761], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/60/0293-01"]}, {"cite": "18 S. E., 953", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "S.E.", "opinion_index": -1}, {"cite": "68 S. C., 26", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4421118], "opinion_index": -1, "case_paths": ["/sc/68/0026-01"]}, {"cite": "56 S. E., 538", "category": "reporters:state_regional", "reporter": "S.E.", "opinion_index": 0}, {"cite": "76 S. C., 9", "category": "reporters:state", "reporter": "S.C.", "case_ids": [4429206], "opinion_index": 0, "case_paths": ["/sc/76/0009-01"]}], "analysis": {"cardinality": 237, "char_count": 3152, "ocr_confidence": 0.616, "pagerank": {"raw": 2.3984282158157867e-07, "percentile": 0.7986193403553643}, "sha256": "566c4bb34be86cc314b3922f5ac968f6e759cea582536196d5ae1745e6e25b6d", "simhash": "1:3635aa28ef8a7401", "word_count": 550}, "last_updated": "2024-02-27T21:13:54.448445+00:00", "provenance": {"date_added": "2019-08-29", "source": "Harvard", "batch": "2018"}, "casebody": {"judges": [], "parties": ["DESCHAMPS v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO."], "opinions": [{"text": "The opinion of the Court was delivered by\nMr. Justice Woods.\nA few days before 24th February, 1907, the plaintiff checked from Charleston, S. C., to Sumter, S. C., on defendant\u2019s railroad, a case containing one Gold Medal Computing Scale, used by him as a sample in his business as a traveling salesman. It does not appear when the scale reached Sumter, but it was in the defendant\u2019s baggage room, at Sumter, on 24th February, 1907, when a fire occurred destroying defendant\u2019s depot, including the baggage room. Plaintiff, in his complaint against the defendant, alleged the scale and case to have been destroyed, and sought to recover their full value, one hundred and twenty-two dollars and fifty cents. The evidence tended to show injury only, and the jury found a verdict for fifty dollars; the defendant appeals.\nThe first four exceptions charge abuse of discretion in the refusal of the Circuit Judge to grant a motion for continuance, made on the ground that William J. Walker, an officer of the Toledo Computing Scales Company, would testify, if present, that the scale belonged to that company and not to the plaintiff; and that, after due diligence, the defendant had not had time to secure the presence of Walker as a witness, or to have his testimony taken. The action was commenced on 1st June, 1907, and was not called for trial until November, 1907. The ownership of the scale was one of the issues made by the pleadings. It was certainly not an abuse of discretion for the Circuit Court to hold that six months was sufficient time to ascertain what would be the testimony of the officers of the scales company, and have it taken in Toledo, Ohio, their place of residence.\nThe instructions complained of in the fifth and sixth exceptions were nothing more than a statement of the rule, well established in this State, that when goods transported by a common carrier arrive at their d\u00e9stination the liability continues to be that of a common carrier until the consignee has a reasonable time to remove them. Brunson v. R. R. Co., 76 S. C., 9, 56 S. E., 538.\nThe judgment of this Court is that the judgment of the Circuit Court be affirmed.", "type": "majority", "author": "Mr. Justice Woods."}], "attorneys": ["Messrs. P. A. Willcox and Mark Reynolds\u25a0, for appellant.", "Mr. L. D. Jennings, contra,"], "corrections": "", "head_matter": "7124\nDESCHAMPS v. ATLANTIC COAST LINE R. R. CO.\n1. Continuance. \u2014 It is not an abuse of discretion to refuse to continue a case on ground that a foreign witness was not present and his testimony had not been secured, the ease having been commenced six months before trial.\n2. Carrier \u2014 Freight.\u2014When goods transported by a common carrier have arrived at their destination the liability continues to be that of carrier until consignee has had reasonable time to remove them.\nBefore Prince;, J., Sumter, November, 1907.\nAffirmed.\nAction by L. W. DesChamps against Atlantic Coast Line Railroad Company. Prom judgment for plaintiff, defendant appeals.\nMessrs. P. A. Willcox and Mark Reynolds\u25a0, for appellant.\nMr. Reynolds cites: As to refusal of continuance: 50' S'. C., 403; 68 S. C., 26.; 18 S. E., 953; 60 S- C., 293; 10 L- R. A., 749. Liability of carrier after delivery of freight: 50 S. C., 129.\nMr. L. D. Jennings, contra,\ncites: Liability of carrier: 76 S. C., 13: 77 S. C., 76.\nApril 9, 1909."}, "file_name": "0236-01", "first_page_order": 248, "last_page_order": 250}